course: dictionary
lesson: 5240
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and jig is one of them. To find the right meaning, look at the words around it. Those words are its context, and they give you clues.
explain: «Dance is moving to music on purpose, and it comes in every size: a fifteen-second phone dance, a breaking battle, an Irish jig, a Ukrainian hopak or a three-hour ballet.» Here jig is a kind of dance, because the sentence is all about dancing to music.
explain: The Rat cried «Hooray!» and then «fell to executing a feeble jig in the snow». He is not dancing to music. He is hopping about in the snow, so a jig here is a lively, hopping dance. Always try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
fact: Jig has more than one meaning, so use the context to choose the right one.
fact: A jig is a fast, lively folk dance, especially from Ireland and Scotland, or the music for it.
fact: To jig is to dance or bounce up and down in a quick, happy way.
fact: A jig is also a tool that holds wood or metal still while you cut or drill it, and you may use one in design and technology.
fact: In fishing, a jig is a lure that is jerked up and down. The phrase «the jig is up» is informal and means the trick has been found out.
exercise: «fell to executing a feeble jig in the snow» What is a jig in this sentence? | A cold path through the snow | *A lively hopping dance | A fishing lure | A tool for cutting wood
exercise: «Dance is moving to music on purpose, and it comes in every size: a fifteen-second phone dance, a breaking battle, an Irish jig, a Ukrainian hopak or a three-hour ballet.» Find and copy the word that names a dance from Ireland. | ballet | hopak | *jig | breaking
exercise: In design and technology, what is a jig for? | Jumping over a fence | Catching fish in a river | *Holding wood or metal in place while you cut or drill it | Making a loud noise
test: Dad says, «The jig is up!» What does the phrase mean? | *The trick has been found out | The dance has finished | Everyone must sit down | The music has stopped
test: Which of these is closest to the verb «to jig»? | Walk slowly through a quiet park | *Bounce up and down quickly and happily | Fall asleep on the sofa | Sit still and read a book
exam: «The fisher gave the line a sharp jig to tempt the fish.» Which meaning fits? | A quiet bird that sings at dusk | A tool for drilling holes | A fast Irish dance | *A lure jerked up and down
